streams, system calls and assembly
==================================

This document covers io/file.c, io/syscall_linux.c, io/string.c and
the two files in assembly/.  Together they are the layer the rest of
the library stands on: everything above them is portable C, everything
here knows the operating system or the architecture.


The streams
-----------

A stream is an opaque object of type stdoc_FILE.  Its layout is an
implementation detail, but internally it is:

    int     fd            operating-system file descriptor
    int     error         sticky failure flag
    int     unbuffered    push every write straight to the kernel
    size_t  length        bytes currently staged
    char    buffer[256]   the staging buffer (STDOC_BUFFER_SIZE)

Three streams exist: stdoc_stdout (fd 1), stdoc_stderr (fd 2) and
stdoc_stdin (fd 0).  stdoc_stdout is buffered; stdoc_stderr is
unbuffered so that it is never reordered with respect to stdout;
stdoc_stdin is reserved for a future input release and is not used by
any function declared in "<libstdoc.h>".

The objects are constant pointers: they stay valid for the whole
program lifetime, but a caller may not rebind stdout to another
object.  A stream bound to a descriptor that does not exist cannot be
obtained through the public interface - test/errors.c builds one on
purpose to exercise what happens next.


Staging
-------

The staging buffer lives inside the stream object, so staging output
performs no allocation.  stdoc_stream_write stages bytes and hands
them to the operating system in a single write when any of three
events happens:

  * the buffer is full,
  * a newline is staged (this is what keeps line-oriented output
    ordered without an exit-time hook, which the freestanding startup
    path does not provide),
  * the write is to an unbuffered stream and the caller has finished.

A payload larger than the buffer is handled by repeated flushes rather
than by an oversized stack copy.

stdoc_stream_flush pushes the staged bytes and clears the buffer; it
succeeds when nothing is staged.  stdoc_stream_discard throws the
staged bytes away without writing them, which is what a failed
formatted call does so that half of a conversion never reaches the
output.

Failure is sticky: once stream->error is set, every further write
fails without touching the system-call layer.


The operating-system layer
--------------------------

io/syscall_linux.c is the only place where Linux system-call numbers
and the "-errno in the return register" convention appear.  The
numbers are written as plain constants - no "<asm/unistd.h>" or other
kernel header is consulted, so the build has no dependency outside
this repository:

    57    close
    63    read
    64    write
    94    exit_group

The wrappers are stdoc_sys_write, stdoc_sys_read, stdoc_sys_close and
stdoc_sys_exit.  The first three return 0 on success and -1 on
failure, mirroring the convention documented for the public functions;
the raw kernel result is classified with a single unsigned comparison
against 4096, because the kernel reports errors as "-errno" in the
range -4095 .. -1 and anything else is a non-negative result.

stdoc_sys_exit uses exit_group rather than exit so that every thread
of the process is terminated.  libstdoc creates no threads, but a
program linked against it may.  It never returns.


The system-call trampoline (assembly/syscall.S)
-----------------------------------------------

The Linux aarch64 system-call ABI differs from the ordinary C calling
convention: the number is carried in x8 rather than x0, the arguments
in x0..x5, and the result comes back in x0.  Arguments are passed in
the same registers the C convention uses, so the trampoline only has
to shift every argument one register to the right, move the number
into place and execute "svc #0":

    stdoc_syscall:  mov x8, x0        /* number       */
                    mov x0, x1        /* arguments    */
                    ...               /* x0 .. x5     */
                    svc #0
                    ret

The raw kernel return value is returned unmodified, so callers
interpret the "-4095 .. -1" range themselves.  Registers x19..x30 are
preserved across the call as the C calling convention requires; the
function touches only argument, result and status registers, so no
explicit save or restore is needed.

Written in GNU assembler (GAS) syntax, as the project conventions
require.


The entry point (assembly/start_aarch64.S)
------------------------------------------

The kernel enters _start with the initial stack layout:

    sp        argc
    sp + 8    argv[0], ..., argv[argc], NULL
              envp[0], ..., NULL

_start clears the frame pointer and the link register so that a stack
unwinder stops cleanly at the bottom of the stack, reads the three
values, calls main(argc, argv, envp) and passes main's return value to
stdoc_sys_exit, which becomes the exit status.

There is deliberately no more here than the ABI requires: no
constructors, no ".init_array" walk, no thread-local storage and no
atexit handling.  Those facilities are documented as unsupported by
this release, which is also why the formatted functions flush
themselves instead of registering an exit-time flush.


The string helpers (io/string.c)
--------------------------------

stdoc_strlen, stdoc_memcpy and stdoc_memset live here because the
library is linked with -nostdlib: it may not call those names from a
host C library, since they would either be missing at link time or
silently resolve to a different implementation than the one under
test.  They are prefixed so they never collide when libstdoc is linked
into a hosted program, and none of them is part of the public API.

The heap allocator in mem/heap.c uses stdoc_memcpy to move block
headers in and out of the arena; see docs/heap for why.
